From: mscharged-port
Subject: Read native float positions for model bounding boxes (issue #18)
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84
Classification: native data transport (Wii byte order), same kind of fix as 0601 and 0617.

GetAABBDimensions builds a model's bounding box from its position stream with
a plain memcpy of three floats. The RLG loader keeps authored vertex streams
in their Wii byte order (CopyRLGWireVertices; Aurora decodes them on the GPU),
so on the host every box was built from byte-swapped floats. A probe of the
box cache in a match read min x -2.5e38, max x 1.6e38 and an infinite extent,
in the Windows and in the Linux build alike.

Thrown items (shells, bananas, Bob-ombs) and hammers draw a planar shadow:
DrawPlanarShadowBounds draws a culled quad 0.1 above the field over the
flattened box, and GX co-planar mode gives the flattened model the depth of
that quad. With a box near 1e38 the quad's corners are infinite or far beyond
float precision, so Aurora keeps the depth plane of an earlier, unrelated
triangle. The item shadows then flickered, were cut along straight lines or
vanished (issue #18, reported on Windows; the Linux build computed the same
garbage boxes).
PlanarShadowDrawable and ChargeShadowDrawable take their radius from the same
boxes.

The native branch reads the same 12-byte rows through NativeRLGFloat3Rows,
which turns each float word into host order. Indices, strides, the s8 branch,
the box cache and the comparisons are unchanged; Wii builds are unchanged.

diff --git a/src/Game/Drawable/DrawableModel.cpp b/src/Game/Drawable/DrawableModel.cpp
